@anon.d1d4bea1a3108f383c23b2c9c4b960e6.388 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [3431 x i8] }> <{ [3431 x i8] c"\0A        Floating point comparison.\0A\0A        Two IEEE 754-2008 floating point numbers, `x` and `y`, relate to each\0A        other in exactly one of four ways:\0A\0A        ```text\0A        == ==========================================\0A        UN Unordered when one or both numbers is NaN.\0A        EQ When `x = y`. (And `0.0 = -0.0`).\0A        LT When `x < y`.\0A        GT When `x > y`.\0A        == ==========================================\0A        ```\0A\0A        The 14 `floatcc` condition codes each correspond to a subset of\0A        the four relations, except for the empty set which would always be\0A        false, and the full set which would always be true.\0A\0A        The condition codes are divided into 7 'ordered' conditions which don't\0A        include UN, and 7 unordered conditions which all include UN.\0A\0A        ```text\0A        +-------+------------+---------+------------+-------------------------+\0A        |Ordered             |Unordered             |Condition                |\0A        +=======+============+=========+============+=========================+\0A        |ord    |EQ | LT | GT|uno      |UN          |NaNs absent / present.   |\0A        +-------+------------+---------+------------+-------------------------+\0A        |eq     |EQ          |ueq      |UN | EQ     |Equal                    |\0A        +-------+------------+---------+------------+-------------------------+\0A        |one    |LT | GT     |ne       |UN | LT | GT|Not equal                |\0A        +-------+------------+---------+------------+-------------------------+\0A        |lt     |LT          |ult      |UN | LT     |Less than                |\0A        +-------+------------+---------+------------+-------------------------+\0A        |le     |LT | EQ     |ule      |UN | LT | EQ|Less than or equal       |\0A        +-------+------------+---------+------------+-------------------------+\0A        |gt     |GT          |ugt      |UN | GT     |Greater than             |\0A        +-------+------------+---------+------------+-------------------------+\0A        |ge     |GT | EQ     |uge      |UN | GT | EQ|Greater than or equal    |\0A        +-------+------------+---------+------------+-------------------------+\0A        ```\0A\0A        The standard C comparison operators, `<, <=, >, >=`, are all ordered,\0A        so they are false if either operand is NaN. The C equality operator,\0A        `==`, is ordered, and since inequality is defined as the logical\0A        inverse it is *unordered*. They map to the `floatcc` condition\0A        codes as follows:\0A\0A        ```text\0A        ==== ====== ============\0A        C    `Cond` Subset\0A        ==== ====== ============\0A        `==` eq     EQ\0A        `!=` ne     UN | LT | GT\0A        `<`  lt     LT\0A        `<=` le     LT | EQ\0A        `>`  gt     GT\0A        `>=` ge     GT | EQ\0A        ==== ====== ============\0A        ```\0A\0A        This subset of condition codes also corresponds to the WebAssembly\0A        floating point comparisons of the same name.\0A\0A        When this instruction compares floating point vectors, it returns a\0A        vector with the results of lane-wise comparisons.\0A\0A        When comparing scalars, the result is:\0A            - `1` if the condition holds.\0A            - `0` if the condition does not hold.\0A\0A        When comparing vectors, the result is:\0A            - `-1` (i.e. all ones) in each lane where the condition holds.\0A            - `0` in each lane where the condition does not hold.\0A        " }>, align 1
